\caption(The original cubic and all ten triangle entries are preserved. The shaded polygon is this example's convex control hull. The red polyline joins 101 native curve samples; it approximates the continuous curve for display.)
\alttext(Four blue controls enclose a red arch. Green and orange interpolation polygons shrink to a gold point at two-one-point-eight-seven-five. Labels identify all original controls and the final midpoint.)

> Derive the recurrence, endpoint property and convex bound

For degree n=m−1 and nonempty controls P₀…P_n, define

\[
P_i^{(0)}=P_i,\qquad
P_i^{(r)}=(1-t)P_i^{(r-1)}+tP_{i+1}^{(r-1)},
\quad 1\le r\le n,\quad 0\le i\le n-r.
\]
\alttext(Each row blends adjacent points from the preceding row with weights one minus t and t. Row r has n minus r plus one points.)

Induction on r expands every intermediate point into a Bernstein sum over
its consecutive original controls:

\[
P_i^{(r)}=\sum_{j=0}^{r}\binom rj(1-t)^{r-j}t^jP_{i+j},
\qquad B(t)=P_0^{(n)}.
\]
\alttext(An intermediate row point is a degree-r Bernstein combination of its r plus one consecutive controls. The last remaining point is the curve value.)

Substitute the two preceding-row expansions into the recurrence. Combining
coefficients uses Pascal's identity, completing the induction. This proves
equivalence with the Bernstein form without requiring the implementation
to compute binomial coefficients or powers. At t=0 every blend keeps its
left parent, and at t=1 it keeps its right parent; the final values are P₀
and P_n. For t in [0,1], weights are nonnegative and sum to one, so all
intermediate points and the curve lie in the controls' convex hull in exact
arithmetic. The original example's interior controls influence the arch;
a general Bézier curve need not avoid every interior control point.

The canonical function retains the original weighted-double update order
(1−t)*left+t*right on a copied vector, moving through each row in increasing
index order. The right parent has not yet been overwritten. Finite t outside
[0,1] still evaluates the polynomial, as the original implementation did,
but the weights cease to be convex and the hull bound no longer follows.
Empty controls retain the legacy (0,0) sentinel, which does not define a
Bézier curve. A singleton is a constant curve. All parameters and controls
must now be finite, including an empty-input parameter; nonfinite weighted
results raise an explicit error instead of propagating silently.

> Subdivision is a native operation, not just a diagram

Fix a split s in [0,1]. The left control net is the triangle's first point
in each row, P₀^(0), P₀^(1), …, P₀^(n). The right net is its last point in
reverse row order, P₀^(n), P₁^(n−1), …, P_n^(0). Their common control is the
same computed B(s). For local u in [0,1], their exact identities are

\[
L(u)=B(su),\qquad R(u)=B(s+(1-s)u).
\]
\alttext(The left subcurve's local parameter u maps to original parameter s times u. The right maps to s plus one minus s times u.)

For the left identity, insert the Bernstein expansion of each diagonal
control into L(u), interchange sums, and use
binom(n,r)binom(r,j)=binom(n,j)binom(n−j,r−j). The remaining binomial sum is
(1−u+u(1−s))^(n−j)=(1−su)^(n−j), leaving the Bernstein coefficients of
B(su). Reverse the original controls and apply the same argument for the
right identity. At s=0 the left net is constant; at s=1 the right is
constant. No division by a zero-length subinterval is needed to extract
these nets. They are equal parameter halves only when s=1/2.

The program splits the original cubic at s=1/4. The core retains only the
two diagonals during this solve, using O(m) storage; no complete triangle
or repeated curve evaluation is needed to produce them. A grid of 101
local values checks both parameter mappings by ordinary native evaluations.
The coloured children and their control nets are shown at equal geometric
scales. A finite grid checks this implementation, while the identity above
explains the continuous mathematical curves.

```{cpp, results="markdown", file="figures/casteljau_subdivision.svg", flags="-std=c++23 -O2 $(pkg-config --cflags --libs datamunge) -Wl,-rpath,$(pkg-config --variable=libdir datamunge)"}
#include <dm/dm.hpp>
int main() {
  std::filesystem::create_directories("build");
  const std::vector<dm::algorithms::PlanarPoint> control{{0,0},{1,2},{3,3},{4,0}};const double s=.25;
  const auto split=dm::algorithms::de_casteljau_subdivide(control,s);
  dm::graphics::Diagram d(170,1100);dm::graphics::DiagramStroke grey;grey.color={148,163,184};grey.width=5;
  std::vector<dm::graphics::DiagramPoint> original;for(const auto& p:dm::algorithms::de_casteljau_sample(control,200))original.push_back({p.x,p.y});d.path(original,grey);
  const std::vector<std::vector<dm::algorithms::PlanarPoint>> nets{split.left,split.right};const std::vector<dm::graphics::RGB> colors{{22,163,74},{234,88,12}};
  for(std::size_t k=0;k<nets.size();++k) {
    dm::graphics::DiagramStroke line;line.color=colors[k];line.width=2;line.dashed=true;
    std::vector<dm::graphics::DiagramPoint> points;for(const auto& p:nets[k])points.push_back({p.x,p.y});d.path(points,line);for(const auto& p:points)d.ellipse(p,.03,.03,colors[k],line);
    line.dashed=false;line.width=3;points.clear();for(const auto& p:dm::algorithms::de_casteljau_sample(nets[k]))points.push_back({p.x,p.y});d.path(points,line);
  }
  double error=0;
  for(int i=0;i<=100;++i) {
    const double u=i/100.;const auto l=dm::algorithms::de_casteljau(split.left,u),r=dm::algorithms::de_casteljau(split.right,u),a=dm::algorithms::de_casteljau(control,s*u),b=dm::algorithms::de_casteljau(control,s+(1-s)*u);
    error=std::max({error,std::abs(l.x-a.x),std::abs(l.y-a.y),std::abs(r.x-b.x),std::abs(r.y-b.y)});
  }
  if(error>1e-12 || split.point_blends!=6)return 1;
  std::print("| control index | left x | left y | right x | right y |\n| ---: | ---: | ---: | ---: | ---: |\n");
  for(std::size_t i=0;i<split.left.size();++i)std::print("| {} | {} | {} | {} | {} |\n",i,split.left[i].x,split.left[i].y,split.right[i].x,split.right[i].y);
  const auto meet=split.left.back();d.ellipse({meet.x,meet.y},.055,.055,{37,99,235})
   .text({2,2.8},"Native split at s=1/4: green left, orange right, grey original",22)
   .text({1.8,.5},std::format("meeting ({},{})",meet.x,meet.y),18)
   .text({2,-.65},"Local maps: left u -> u/4; right u -> 1/4 + 3u/4",21)
   .save_svg("build/casteljau_subdivision.svg");
  std::print("\nTwo native nets have {} and {} controls; actual point blends {}. Maximum coordinate identity residual over 101 local parameters: {:.9g}.\n",split.left.size(),split.right.size(),split.point_blends,error);
}
