> Absolute sensitivity does not promise small relative error

At a fixed t in [0,1], perturb every control by at most ε in a chosen norm.
Because Bernstein weights are nonnegative and sum to one,

\[
\|\widetilde B(t)-B(t)\|
\le\sum_i w_i(t)\|\widetilde P_i-P_i\|\le\varepsilon.
\]
\alttext(The curve displacement is bounded by the largest control displacement, because its nonnegative Bernstein weights sum to one.)

This is a statement about control-data perturbations in exact arithmetic;
it is not a bound on all floating arithmetic errors. If only the second
control's y coordinate increases by one in the original cubic, the exact
y displacement is its Bernstein weight 3t(1−t)². The program compares this
formula with two sampled curves. Its continuous maximum is 4/9 at t=1/3;
the display grid need not include that exact parameter.

Relative conditioning is different. A linear curve with controls x=10^16
and x=−10^16 has midpoint zero. Changing the latter by two, a relative
control change of 2×10^−16, makes the midpoint one. Relative change compared
with the original zero answer is undefined. This is cancellation in the
problem, not evidence that repeated interpolation has failed. Convexity
and “numerically stable” must not be read as a universal guarantee of small
relative error at a nearly cancelled answer. The finite weighted-sum
implementation can also reject extrapolations whose intermediate products
overflow even when some alternative algebraic rearrangement would be finite.

```{cpp, results="markdown", file="figures/casteljau_sensitivity.svg", flags="-std=c++23 -O2 $(pkg-config --cflags --libs datamunge) -Wl,-rpath,$(pkg-config --variable=libdir datamunge)"}
#include <dm/dm.hpp>
int main() {
  std::filesystem::create_directories("build");
  const std::vector<dm::algorithms::PlanarPoint> control{{0,0},{1,2},{3,3},{4,0}};auto changed=control;changed[1].y+=1;
  const auto base=dm::algorithms::de_casteljau_sample(control),perturbed=dm::algorithms::de_casteljau_sample(changed);
  std::vector<double> t_values,effect,theory;double residual=0,max_effect=0;
  std::print("| parameter t | native y displacement | Bernstein prediction |\n| ---: | ---: | ---: |\n");
  for(std::size_t i=0;i<base.size();++i) {
    const double t=i/100.,delta=perturbed[i].y-base[i].y,prediction=3*t*(1-t)*(1-t);
    t_values.push_back(t);effect.push_back(delta);theory.push_back(prediction);residual=std::max(residual,std::abs(delta-prediction));max_effect=std::max(max_effect,std::abs(delta));
    if(i%25==0)std::print("| {} | {} | {} |\n",t,delta,prediction);
  }
  const auto zero=dm::algorithms::de_casteljau({{1e16,0},{-1e16,0}},.5),one=dm::algorithms::de_casteljau({{1e16,0},{-1e16+2,0}},.5),outside=dm::algorithms::de_casteljau({{0,0},{1,1}},2);
  if(residual>1e-12 || max_effect>1 || zero.x!=0 || one.x!=1 || outside.x!=2)return 1;
  std::print("\nOne-control displacement: grid maximum {:.6f}, theory residual {:.9g}, control perturbation bound 1.\n",max_effect,residual);
  std::print("Cancellation control: midpoint before {}; after a +2 control change {}. Finite extrapolation of the unit line at t=2: {}.\n",zero,one,outside);
  auto p=dm::graphics::RPlot::create();
  p.line(t_values,effect,"native difference",{37,99,235},2).points(t_values,theory,"Bernstein weight",{31,41,55},2)
   .legend({"native difference","Bernstein weight"},{{37,99,235},{31,41,55}})
   .title("Move one control by one: the curve moves by its Bernstein weight")
   .x_label("parameter t").y_label("change in curve y coordinate")
   .x_limits(0,1).y_limits(0,.6).size(1100,570).save_svg("build/casteljau_sensitivity.svg");
}
