> Independent controls and practical scope

Nine new tests pass AddressSanitizer and UndefinedBehaviorSanitizer. Three
hundred seeded integer control nets of degrees zero through twelve are
checked at all nine eighth-grid parameters: an independent signed-128-bit
Bernstein numerator checks every point of every actual triangle row. The
chosen small integers and dyadic degrees fit exact double arithmetic, so
these checks compare exact values. Another hundred nets, five split values
including zero and one, and seventeen local parameters check both subdivision
identities and diagonal ownership. One thousand non-dyadic random controls
check a separate long-double Bernstein sum, affine transformation, unit-
interval bounding boxes and finite extrapolation. Tests retain the original
cubic, all triangle entries, endpoints, sample-grid ordering and tiny/equal/
descending intervals, empty/singleton behavior, ownership, complete caps,
count overflow, validation precedence, explicit arithmetic overflow and the
controlled sensitivity/cancellation examples. Native de Boor reduction and
existing interpolation/format tests check compatibility with neighbouring APIs.

These finite controls do not turn floating rows into exact convex-hull
certificates or provide a general bound on relative arithmetic error.
Subdivision supplies reusable control nets, and uniform sampling supplies
complete display grids; neither implements adaptive stopping or guarantees
a display polyline's chord error. No external dataset is needed: the original
cubic teaches the geometry, and exact arithmetic/perturbation/degree controls
test the stated identities and limits directly.

> References

- Paul de Casteljau (1985), *Formes à pôles*, Mathématiques et CAO, volume 2, Hermès, Paris. [Bibliographic record](https://bibliotheque.map.cnrs.fr/index.php?id=86&lvl=notice_display). The original author's treatment of the construction.
- Gerald Farin (2002), *Curves and Surfaces for CAGD: A Practical Guide*, fifth edition, ISBN 1-55860-737-4. [Author's contents and materials](https://www.farinhansford.com/books/cagd/toc.html). De Casteljau, Bernstein and subdivision topics provide the mathematical framework; this implementation retains the repository's double weighted-sum update order.
- Nicholas M. Patrikalakis, Takashi Maekawa and Wonjoon Cho, *Shape Interrogation for Computer Aided Design and Manufacturing*, [Bézier evaluation and subdivision](https://web.mit.edu/hyperbook/Patrikalakis-Maekawa-Cho/node13.html). The two control-diagonal interpretation matches the subdivision operation exposed here.
